You can use the `chessboard` package in LaTeX to draw a chessboard and specify the position using a FEN string. The FEN describes the arrangement of pieces on the board, as well as additional information such as whose turn it is and castling availability.

Below is a complete example:

```latex
\documentclass{article}

\usepackage{chessboard}

\begin{document}

\setchessboard{showmover=false}

\chessboard[
  setfen=r1bqkbnr/pppp1ppp/2n5/4p3/2B1P3/5N2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQK2R w KQkq - 2 4
]

\end{document}
```

In this example, the `setfen` option is used to specify the position in FEN notation. The `chessboard` package automatically renders the board according to that position.

If you want to show an empty board instead, you can simply write:

```latex
\chessboard
```

The package also supports many customization options, such as board size, colors, labels, and arrows.

